# The layers whose window permits working out a time slack from a clearance. One row per nameplate
# whose window leaves the spare spread evenly across the period.
#
# A rule stated the way it reads
#   This is the positive form of a rule usually written as a negation, and the positive form is the
#   one that reads. A derived time slack is the clearance, and reading it as demand that survives
#   being held assumes the spare is spread evenly across the period: only then is the wait it
#   implies the wait anybody has. A window that is only part of the period says it is not (the
#   machine runs 02:00 to 05:00), so the average is over a base that does not exist. `unmeasured`
#   says nobody knows whether it is, which is an assumption dressed as a calculation.
#
# Two reasons, not two documents
#   Exactly two reasons license the derivation, "it runs the whole period" and "the question has no
#   period", across however many layers file one of them.
#
# The first reason is never read out of a blank
#   That is why this file exists. `window_absent IN ('none', 'notApplicable')` reads `none` as "the
#   window is the whole period": a number, filed as though it were a nothing. The blank carries no
#   size and no origin, so read that way a line that cannot be stopped and a line somebody staffed
#   round the clock license this derivation identically, and neither says so. The licence is read
#   off a filed window, where the two spellings come to one.
#
# The period arrives by a semi-join
#   This asks "is there a period equal to this window's unit" and uses no value from the answer, so
#   `EXISTS` states the question it means. It is also a guard. Let `pm.claim.owns` carry an element
#   name rather than a path, and `Demand/amount` and `Nameplate/amount` are one `pm:amount` to
#   `units/with_a_period.sqlc`, which then returns two rows per layer: a plain join fans out, a
#   semi-join hides it. `owns` is a path, and the relation is one row per layer.
#
# A whole period is quoted as `1`
#   A whole period is quoted as `1` in the period's own unit. `1 week` against a period of `week` is
#   the whole period, read without dividing anything; `5 days` against the same period is only part
#   of it. No unit is converted here, on purpose: units are never guessed at in SQL here, and a
#   licence decided by a `LIKE` is a reader's judgement dressed as a query.
-- pm:Divisibility/window: absent notApplicable, or filed as one whole period.
SELECT w.filing, w.layer,
       CASE WHEN w.window_absent IS NOT NULL THEN 'the question has no denominator'
            ELSE 'it runs the whole period' END AS licensed_because
FROM (
    :compose(layers/windows.sqlc)
) w
WHERE w.window_absent = 'notApplicable'
   OR (w.window_low = 1 AND w.window_low = w.window_high
       AND EXISTS (
           SELECT 1
           FROM (
               :compose(units/with_a_period.sqlc)
           ) p
           WHERE p.filing = w.filing AND p.layer = w.layer
             AND p.period = w.window_unit
       ))
